<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pre-Columbian era</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pre-Columbian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The pre-Columbian era refers to the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> before the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> in 1492 and subsequent European colonisation. It saw the rise of complex civilisations such as the Maya, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>. The Aztec Empire was among the most powerful pre-Columbian states before its conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pre-columbian-era.jpg" alt="Pre-Columbian era" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fcb981 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Castillo_Stitch_2008_Edit_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The term, also called the pre-contact, pre-Hispanic or, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, pre-Cabraline era, spans the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> from the initial peopling in the Upper Paleolithic to the onset of European colonisation. It covers Indigenous cultures before significant European influence, which in some cases did not come until decades or even centuries after 1492. Many of these societies developed permanent settlements, cities, agriculture, monumental architecture, major earthworks and complex hierarchies. Some had declined by the time of the first permanent European colonies and are known mainly through archaeology and oral histories.</p><p>Before archaeology developed in the 19th century, historians relied on the records of the conquerors and early European travellers. The Maya kept written records, many of which were destroyed by Christian Europeans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a>, though a few survive and others were transcribed or translated into Spanish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Asian Paleo-Indians are thought to have entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> by the Bering Land Bridge and possibly along the coast, and spread south through an ice-free corridor and along the Pacific coast. Genetic evidence supports several migrations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the Woodland period lasted from roughly 1000 BCE to 1000 CE, and the Adena and Hopewell traditions built monumental earthworks and wide trade networks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a> appeared around 1000 CE and built great mound complexes; Cahokia may have reached a population of over 20,000, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monks-mound">Monks Mound</a> remains the largest earthen construction of the prehistoric Americas. In the Southwest the Ancestral Puebloans built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> and the great houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> produced a series of related civilisations over about 3,000 years. The Olmec, the earliest known, consolidated power at San Lorenzo between 1600 and 1500 BCE. Teotihuacan, first settled about 300 BCE, had become a metropolis by 150 CE, while the Maya flourished between 250 and 650 CE and developed a script, mathematics and a calendar. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> later led the Aztec Triple Alliance with Tetxcoco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> the Norte Chico or Caral-Supe civilisation emerged around 3200 BCE on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and is regarded as the oldest known civilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Later states included the Moche, Wari and Tiwanaku. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> dominated the Andes from 1438 to 1533, ruling some 9 to 14 million people connected by a 40,000-kilometre road system. Early inhabitants also bred wild teosinte into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and over two-thirds of the food crops grown worldwide are native to the Americas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pre-Columbian era” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
